==Portuguese==
[[Image:Lemon and lime.jpg|thumb|{{uxi|pt|Um '''limão''' à esquerda e uma lima à direita.|A lemon on the left and a lime on the right.}}]]

===Etymology===
{{etymon|pt|id=lemon|:bor|ar:لَيْمُون|tree=1}}
{{bor+|pt|ar|لَيْمُون}} via {{bor|pt|xaa|-}}, from {{der|pt|fa|لیمون//لیمو|tr=limun, limu}}, from {{der|pt|sa|निम्बू}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{pt-IPA}}
* {{hyphenation|pt|li|mão}}

===Noun===
{{pt-noun|m}}

# [[lemon]] {{gloss|citrus fruit}}
#: {{syn|pt|limão-siciliano<q:Brazil>}}
# {{lb|pt|Brazil}} [[lime#Noun_3|lime]] {{gloss|citrus fruit}}
#: {{syn|pt|lima}}
#: {{hypo|pt|limão-taiti}}
# {{lb|pt|South Brazil}} [[lemandarin]], [[rangpur]]
#: {{syn|pt|limão-cravo}}

====Usage notes====
* In Brazil, unless otherwise specified, the variants {{vern|persian lime}} ({{taxlink|Citrus × latifolia|nothospecies}}) and {{l|en|Key lime}} ({{taxlink|Citrus × aurantiifolia|nothospecies}}) are assumed. In southern regions, {{l|en|lemandarin}} ({{taxlink|Citrus × limonia|nothospecies}}) may be assumed as well.
* Brazilians also consider a {{l|en|lemon}} ({{taxfmt|Citrus limon|species}}) to be a {{m|pt|limão}}; specifically, {{m|pt|limão siciliano}}.
* Non-Brazilians distinguish {{m|pt|lima||lime}} from {{m|pt|limão}}.

